n agriculture  commuters traveling to work truck  drivers delivering goods and of course  medical professionals Germany also  announced on Monday the latest extension  to its 1.1 trillion euro coronavirus  crisis package a guarantee of 100% loans  to small and medium sized businesses of  up to eight hundred thousand euros these  middle stand companies are widely seen  as the heart of the German economy  anyone who is economically active last  year those who registered turnover those  who recorded normal economic activity  and made a profit they can get three  months turnover as a loan from the bank  which in this case will be 100%  financially underwritten by us  that means the banks will not have to do  normal financial risk assessments this  100% guarantee means the process will be  completed very quickly now Germany's  European neighbors too are looking to  Berlin to see whether Merkel's concerns  might also turn into financial support  for them  and here's DW correspondent Baird auriga  at explaining exactly the kind of  financial support Germany's European  neighbors are looking for 9 European  countries among them Italy France and  Spain are demanding common debt euro  bonds or corona bonds but Germany and  Austria and other wealthy countries say  we cannot afford this to finance common  euro bonds or common debt because we  don't know how we will come out of this  unprecedented crisis but many experts  and economists Ian Brussels say the 500  billion euros now on the table are not  enough you have to do more come up with  more money and Italy is asking quite  bluntly what is the EU for the  they cannot help us now DW political  correspondent Hans Brandt joins me now  from our parliamentary studios Hans what  is the EU for if it cannot help us now  that's the cry from some countries  angular Merkel acknowledges this is the  biggest test the EU has ever faced so  how much pressure is there on Germany to  contribute more money yes the pressure  on Germany is huge also within the  German political spectrum there are many  political parties and politicians here  in Germany that are pushing the  government urging the government to  agree to these corona bonds as they are  called but we have to caution that it's  not a matter of Germany giving money we  are talking here about loans that are to  be issued and about Germany and other  countries all countries in the European  Union guaranteeing those learn says not  actually money that's being kind of  pushed across the table it's a guarantee  for loans but because the German economy  is by far the strongest in Europe if  these loans should end up not being  repaid or in some sense getting  difficult it will be Germany that ends  up in some sense guaranteeing these  loans together with other countries so  it's that guarantee that the Germans are  concerned about the German Foreign  Minister and the economics minister have  recently published opinion pieces in the  series of European newspapers offering  their alternative they are saying that a  common European Stability Mechanism  which is already in place should be used  and that the rules for giving that money  to various countries should be relaxed  so Germany is hoping in some things to  get out of this bind and still be able  to help other countries  speaking of relaxations the Chancellor  also said it's too early to set a date  for the relaxation of social distancing  why is Germany not following Austria  into issuing a timed programme of dates  for restrictions to be lifted well both  Chancellor Merkel and members of her  cabinet for instance the economics  minister only this morning have been  stressing that it's much too early for  Germany even to talk about a date when  this might happen when the relaxation  might happen they are emphasizing that  the restrictions will remain in place at  until the 19th of April in other words  ten days or so and only when that date  approaches will they start talking about  possible relaxations it has to do with  the fact that there is a feeling that  the situation in Germany is not under  control completely yet and also with the  fact that this is holiday times here in  Europe in Germany especially there's the  Easter weekend coming up and one wants  to discourage people from leaving their  home from relaxing their guard from  letting down their guard from meeting  other people and so on so in this under  these circumstances the feeling is that  things have to remain under control and  strictly controlled hands broad speaking  to us from our parliamentary studios  thank you very much  let's get you the latest developments  related to the global la coruna virus  pandemic China has reported no new  deaths from the corona virus over the  past 24 hours and the 32 new cases the  country did report were people who  returned from overseas Israel has  announced a complete lockdown over the  upcoming Passover holiday here in  Germany the number of confirmed  coronavirus infections has passed a  hundred thousand the fourth highest  caseload in the world and Japan has  declared a state of emergency in Tokyo  and six other regions to combat the  outbreak Japan has nearly 4,000  confirmed cases of kovat 19 and 92  deaths from it for more I'm joined by  journalist Sonia Blaschka in Tokyo Sonia  what does a state of emergency mean in  concrete terms it means that the  governors have the authority to call on  people to stay home and for businesses  to close however they don't have they  cannot give any penalties and it's not  like a european-style lockdown so to  speak so people are still free to move  it's still basically more or less a very  strong appeal and if you compare it to  Europe the the situation here is very  different  much less restricted it's it's much more  like regular life and I'm hoping that  with the state of emergency this sense  of crisis so to speak will will change  and it affects the two main regions in  Japan it affects Tokyo and the greater  Tokyo area with 35 million people and  also the greater osaka and kyoto area  before for well over a month right sonya  japan initially seemed to be on top of  the coronavirus response but infections  have surged recently what went wrong I  think in the beginning what helped was  the proximity to China  there was a very early in the crisis a  sense that that people really need to  take care be careful and all that  schools were closed relatively early but  I think because it kind of took so long  and the the number of infections stay  relatively low people maybe forgot about  a little bit or starts to get a little  complacent and then the cherry blossom  season happened we had a super nice  weekend in in March a lot of people went  through the parks despite pleas not to  do so and I think the results is what  we're seeing now and Prime Minister  Shinzo Abe a has also announced a huge  stimulus package what can you tell us  about that yes he has announced a  stimulus package which amounts to 108  trillion yen which is 916 billion euros  and it's equivalent to 20 percent of the  Japanese economic output compared to  Germany Germany has invested around five  percent the the goal is obviously to  prevent recession from happening and  maybe just turn it into some sort of a  depression they will give money to  families whose income has dropped by  over 50% they will give them three  hundred thousand yen that's about more  than four hundred euros and also maybe  around 80 euros per child then also  money for companies 2 million yen so  that's what the stimulus package model  is in place  Sonia Blaschka in Tokyo thank you very  much for that you're welcome
 

